### A manual, UNREVIEWED necessary lemma

Suppose `m>=3`, `a_s(z),b_s(z)` are scalar monic polynomials of z-degrees `m,n=2m-1`, homogeneous for `wt(s)=1,wt(z)=D`; let

    A=a_s(R)+F,  B=b_s(R)+q_s(R)F+G,
    q=quotient(b',a'),  rho=b'-a'q,
    1<=j=ord_s F<=mD-1,  ker[H,-]=k[H].

Assume the full source Jacobian has s-order `L>2j`. The following argument allows every lower scalar B kernel to be chosen; it does not assume a fixed previously prescribed kernel gauge.

Since `deg_z rho<=m-2`, homogeneity gives, for a nonzero coefficient of `z^i`,

    ord_s rho_i=(n-1-i)D >= mD > j.                 (3.1)

Euclidean division uses only the fixed unitm, never division byR orH. Thus15z's exact identity

    [A,B]=[R,T]+[F,G],
    T=a'(R)G-rho(R)F-q'(R)F^2/2

has `rho F` strictly later than2j. If G has a first coefficient at `ell<2j`, its bracket equation is `[H,mH^(m-1)G_ell]=0`, so `G_ell` is a scalar polynomial inH. Combined homogeneity makes it a single scalar power. It is removable by the corresponding scalar B kernel. Recomputing q changes qF only at orders at least `ell+j`, never at an earlier order; all Euclidean-remainder coefficients still satisfy(3.1). Induction through the FINITELY many orders below2j therefore gives `ord G>=2j`. This retains, rather than sets to zero, all scalar choices.

At order2j the COMPLETE equation gives

    mH^(m-1)G_(2j) - Kappa H^(m-2)F_j^2 in k[H],
    Kappa=(2m-1)(m-1)/(2m) !=0.

The scalar centralizer term, if present, has H-exponent `3m-2-2j/D`. After removing `H^(m-2)`, its exponent is `2m-2j/D>0`; it is an integer whenever that term exists. Otherwise the scalar term is zero. Reduction moduloH consequently gives **H divides F_j squared**. Exceptional scalar orders have not been discarded.

For a homogeneous target of ordinary degree t, `L=(3m-1)D-2-t`. The easily checked sufficient inequality `t<(m-1)D` implies `L>2j` for every allowedj. This includes the numerical degree-seven covered receiver target `t=3`, but is not a proof that an entire F9 family reaches that receiver.

This lemma is a small new-to-this-packet derivation of the scalar-gap window, with KNOWN product-rule/centralizer machinery. It is not a novelty claim against the literature or uncharged campaign reports.15z does not refute it: its primitive-root exponents21/35 do not satisfy `n=2m-1`, and its rho order60 precedes j79. Conversely, one cannot restore(3.1) merely by relabeling that pair3/5: that would replace the closed primitive root by a nonclosed proper power.

